content.text:
Phase 3 is built: `/mcp` now routes protocol 2026-07-28 clients to a new stateless lane and 2025-era clients to the existing session lane. `bun run verify` is green (745 unit, 60 web, 571 integration) and a local dry-run build of the worker succeeds. All three phases are uncommitted and undeployed, and nothing has run against a real Claude client yet.

## How it works

- **Routing:** each request is classified with the MCP SDK's own `isLegacyRequest`. `initialize`, session-id requests, GET and DELETE go to `McpSessionDO` untouched; everything else goes to the stateless handler.
- **Per-request server:** the stateless lane builds a fresh server for each request and registers only what that request addresses. A `tools/call` registers just the one tool it names.
- **Checked fresh every request:** the account's status (a suspended user is cut off on the next request), the upstream catalogue, and credentials (a just-re-authorized upstream works immediately).
- **Same surface as the session lane:**
  - the same 14 built-ins in the same order;
  - the same skill prompts and `/draft-skill`;
  - the same doc and skill resources;
  - the same upstream tool descriptions, errors and scrubbing.
- **Usage:** recorded as `stateless` with the client's name, so the Protocol lanes panel will show both lanes side by side.
- **First-result playbook hint:** once per upstream per user and client, re-armed after 12 hours, since there is no session to count. This needs migration `0042_mcp_hint_ledger.sql`; a nightly job prunes old rows.
- **Kill switch:** setting `MCP_STATELESS_LANE=off` sends everything back to the session lane. It is on by default.

`reload_upstreams` on the stateless lane has nothing to reload, so it returns the current upstream list and says so.

## Tests

`test/integration/stateless-lane.test.ts` drives the real router with a real 2026-07-28 client against D1 (19 tests). They cover:

- routing in both directions and the kill switch;
- the tool list being limited to what the caller may use;
- an ungranted upstream, an ACL-locked tool and a made-up tool being indistinguishable;
- a thrown upstream error reaching the agent as a generic code with no credential or internal address;
- the hint firing once and re-arming;
- suspension taking effect on the next request;
- prompts and resources being served.

## Not verified

These need a real deployment:

- the OAuth provider handing identity to the new handler in production;
- usage rows landing through the real queue;
- Claude Desktop and claude.ai actually choosing the stateless lane;
- how long `tools/list` takes with the roughly 460 tools you have on yukitools.

## Before any release

- **Canary first.** Every hosted-connector user moves to the new lane the moment a tenant is released, because Claude probes 2026-07-28 first. Release the dev tenant and connect your own Desktop to it before yukitools.
- **Migrations.** `0041` and `0042` must be applied before the worker is deployed.
- **Session lane refactor.** Phases 2 and 3 moved code the session lane uses (built-ins, prompt/resource registration, description and catalogue helpers). Its existing tests pass, but the Durable Object itself is only proven on a release.

I also updated the architecture pointer in `CLAUDE.md` to describe the two lanes. The lane router is in [handler.ts](apps/worker/src/mcp/stateless/handler.ts), the factory in [build-server.ts](apps/worker/src/mcp/stateless/build-server.ts), and the upstream path in [upstream-tools.ts](apps/worker/src/mcp/stateless/upstream-tools.ts).
